<section class="weekly-section weekly-section--lead">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">Opening finding</div>
<h2>DeepSeek Harness advisory says local processes could answer human approval requests</h2>
<p class="weekly-lead-p">VulnCheck's 4 November advisory reports that DeepSeek Harness exposed an unauthenticated control plane through which local processes could invoke agent shell tools and bypass human approval.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup> Approval identifiers exposed in a session stream were sufficient to answer pending requests, so the presence of an approval step did not establish reviewer authority.</p>
<p>The advisory establishes a vulnerability, not a confirmed enterprise breach. Exploitation dates and affected organizations are not established.</p>
<div class="weekly-figure"><div class="weekly-figcaption-label">Explanatory diagram / Advisory-described failure</div><div class="weekly-flow weekly-flow--3"><div class="weekly-flow-step"><span class="weekly-flow-num">01 / LOCAL PROCESS</span><b>No authenticated reviewer</b><span>A local process reaches the agent control plane.</span></div><div class="weekly-flow-step"><span class="weekly-flow-num">02 / APPROVAL RESPONSE</span><b>Identifiers accepted</b><span>The exposed session identifiers can answer a pending request.</span></div><div class="weekly-flow-step weekly-flow-step--outcome"><span class="weekly-flow-num">03 / EXECUTION</span><b>Shell tools exposed</b><span>The advisory reports command execution as the victim user.</span></div></div><div class="weekly-limit">An approval response must prove current authority for the exact action, not merely reference a pending request.</div><p class="weekly-figcaption">Source and credit: Jochanni Labs explanatory visual based on <a href="https://www.vulncheck.com/advisories/deepseek-harness-unauthenticated-rpc-command-execution" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">VulnCheck, 4 November 2026</a>. Not a screenshot, reproduced exploit or customer system.</p></div>
</div>
</section>
