<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">02 / Incidents and Enforcement Gaps</div>
<h2>One new advisory and one research update</h2>
<p>New, demonstrated vulnerability: DeepSeek Harness, CVE-2026-82033. The reported consequence is arbitrary command execution as the victim user, including approval bypass. The advisory identifies versions before 0.1.2-alpha.2; its commit references need clarification.<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup></p>
<p>Update, demonstrated vulnerabilities: Cymulate's 3 November synthesis connects earlier findings into two researcher-demonstrated chains reaching privileged Azure and GCP identities. Execution-sensitive configuration writes and workspace executable launches crossed trust boundaries without the required approval. These are demonstrations, not reported customer compromises; underlying test dates are unspecified.<sup><a href="#s2">2</a></sup></p>
<p>In this reviewed case set: two vulnerability reports, one new and one update. No confirmed unauthorized production execution or blocked production attempt was established. These counts are not an incident rate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">03 / Domains Requiring Enforcement</div>
<h2>Check the action where it changes the system</h2>
<div class="weekly-table-wrap"><table class="weekly-table"><thead><tr><th>Domain / source</th><th>Exact action</th><th>Authority check belongs</th><th>If absent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Agent host<sup><a href="#s1">1</a></sup></td><td>Execute a shell command.</td><td>Command executor, before process creation.</td><td>Commands run as the victim user.</td></tr><tr><td>Development / cloud<sup><a href="#s2">2</a></sup></td><td>Write execution configuration or change a setup script.</td><td>Configuration write and launch boundaries.</td><td>Attacker code runs and exposes cloud credentials.</td></tr><tr><td>CRM<sup><a href="#s3">3</a></sup></td><td>Change an account owner.</td><td>Record-update handler, binding account and new owner.</td><td>An unapproved ownership change can execute.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact</div>
<h2>A fresh approval question needs its own answer</h2>
<p>LangGraph's released changes give successive interrupts distinct identifiers. Maintainer tests report rejection of a retried answer once the next question is pending. Strict rejection is opt-in, with documented checkpoint exceptions; this is not proof of single-use downstream execution or authorized human review.<sup><a href="#s5">5</a>, <a href="#s6">6</a></sup></p>
<p>Algen describes an executor refusing calls without covering approval, storing exact parameters and recording idempotency keys. Interrupted writes remain indeterminate rather than automatically retried. Its example is synthetic, and the runtime is a single-node prerelease.<sup><a href="#s3">3</a></sup></p>
<p>Decision Governance is the category. The Decision Authority Layer is the runtime enforcement layer; DAL-X is the application Jochanni Labs is building. System access does not establish authority to execute a specific action. Enterprise trigger logic identifies actions requiring an authority decision.</p>
<p>Conditional assessment: DAL-style enforcement could stop an unauthorized shell command only if triggers cover it, reviewer authority is verified independently of exposed request identifiers, approval binds the exact payload, and every relevant execution path rejects missing, mismatched, expired or consumed authority before launch. This would not make an approved command correct.<sup><a href="#s8">8</a></sup></p>
<p class="weekly-status">DAL-X status: the layer site reports real-system sandbox execution with no customer deployment. The application site still describes simulation with production controls incomplete. Neither establishes an enterprise pilot or production enforcement.<sup><a href="#s9">9</a>, <a href="#s10">10</a></sup></p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">05 / Enterprise Check and Next Week's Watch</div>
<h2>Test the reviewer and the reused response</h2>
<p>Shell execution: submit approval from an identity lacking the required mandate, then retry an old response against a new command. Effective control requires executor rejection, no process launch, and matching authority and outcome records.</p>
<p>Watch for clarified DeepSeek patch references and LangGraph tests binding approval to exact tool arguments. Seek executor-level evidence behind Sysdig's blocking claims.</p>
<div class="weekly-question">Can your command executor distinguish a valid approver from a process holding an approval identifier?</div>
